"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,711\nThe Learning Brain. Series 6: Neuromyths\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,391\nEpisode 5\n\n3\n00:00:07,471 --> 00:00:09,611\nBrain-training games\n\n4\n00:00:12,411 --> 00:00:14,124\nTen minutes a day of brain games,\n\n5\n00:00:14,144 --> 00:00:15,648\nand you will think faster,\n\n6\n00:00:15,812 --> 00:00:17,734\nremember more and focus better.\n\n7\n00:00:18,071 --> 00:00:19,306\nThat was the promise.\n\n8\n00:00:19,562 --> 00:00:21,044\nWhat did the evidence show?\n\n9\n00:00:21,214 --> 00:00:24,235\nBy 2014, scientists were split.\n\n10\n00:00:24,736 --> 00:00:27,839\nOne open letter, from about seventy researchers,\n\n11\n00:00:28,078 --> 00:00:30,186\nobjected to brain training claims.\n\n12\n00:00:30,206 --> 00:00:32,537\nAnother, from more than a hundred,\n\n13\n00:00:32,557 --> 00:00:33,631\ndefended them.\n\n14\n00:00:33,801 --> 00:00:44,635\nSo in 2016, Daniel Simons of the University of Illinois and six colleagues reviewed more than one hundred thirty studies cited in support of brain training.\n\n15\n00:00:45,232 --> 00:00:47,834\nThey asked: how big was the study,\n\n16\n00:00:48,036 --> 00:00:49,899\nwas there a proper control group,\n\n17\n00:00:50,285 --> 00:00:52,419\nand did the results match the claims?\n\n18\n00:00:52,589 --> 00:00:55,861\nMany studies were small or lacked good controls.\n\n19\n00:00:56,265 --> 00:01:00,070\nThe better studies showed that people got better at the trained games.\n\n20\n00:01:00,419 --> 00:01:05,220\nBut there was little evidence that the gains transferred to everyday thinking,\n\n21\n00:01:05,331 --> 00:01:06,256\nat work or at school.\n\n22\n00:01:06,854 --> 00:01:09,811\nThe authors did not say training can never work,\n\n23\n00:01:10,107 --> 00:01:13,454\nonly that the evidence did not yet support the claims.\n\n24\n00:01:13,624 --> 00:01:15,371\nRegulators noticed too.\n\n25\n00:01:15,961 --> 00:01:26,253\nIn 2016, the company behind Lumosity agreed to pay two million dollars to settle Federal Trade Commission charges of deceptive advertising.\n\n26\n00:01:26,785 --> 00:01:29,586\nThe company has since scaled back its claims.\n\n27\n00:01:29,756 --> 00:01:34,327\nThe grain of truth: practice really does improve what you practice.\n\n28\n00:01:34,621 --> 00:01:37,150\nThat is exactly why the games get easier.\n\n29\n00:01:37,528 --> 00:01:40,785\nThe trouble is the leap from a game to everything else.\n\n30\n00:01:40,955 --> 00:01:42,147\nDo this instead.\n\n31\n00:01:42,522 --> 00:01:45,873\nHave students practice the actual skills your course needs,\n\n32\n00:01:45,943 --> 00:01:48,596\nwith feedback, in varied contexts.\n\n33\n00:01:48,932 --> 00:01:53,328\nBuild transfer on purpose by showing the same idea in different settings.\n\n34\n00:01:53,730 --> 00:01:58,782\nOne tip to share with students: practice the actual skill you want to improve.\n\n35\n00:01:58,952 --> 00:02:00,887\nYou get better at what you practice.\n\n36\n00:02:01,066 --> 00:02:02,814\nSo practice what matters.\n\n"